    {
      "page_title": "O(N) Traps, Memory & Unique Visitors",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The O(N) Watch List"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Commands that block",
          "items": [
            "`KEYS`, `SMEMBERS`, `HGETALL`",
            "`LRANGE 0 -1`, `ZRANGE 0 -1`, `SORT`",
            "`DEL` on a huge collection",
            "`FLUSHALL` without `ASYNC`"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Rule of thumb",
          "text": "Any command whose result size grows with the collection needs a `LIMIT`/`COUNT` bound or a `*SCAN` cursor. \"Fast\" means \"bounded N\"."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Memory, Small To Large"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "flow",
          "spec": {
            "nodes": [
              "intset",
              "listpack",
              "hashtable",
              "skiplist"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "1M user flags",
            "Memory"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "1M string keys",
              "~80 MB overhead"
            ],
            [
              "10k hashes × 100",
              "a few MB"
            ],
            [
              "one bitmap",
              "125 KB"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Same data, three shapes — pick by what you must query",
            "Small collections are dramatically cheaper per element",
            "Big-O is per command; latency is per server",
            "O(N) with N = 10^6 is milliseconds of blocking for everyone"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Counter-example",
          "text": "Compact encodings are linear-scan. A `listpack` hash of 500 fields is slower per lookup than a `hashtable`. The thresholds exist for a reason."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "What You Graduate To"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "streams: append-only logs with consumer groups",
            "geospatial (sorted-set-backed): radius queries",
            "JSON module: nested documents",
            "Search module: secondary indexes"
