    {
      "page_title": "Pipelines, Transactions & Lua",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "redis",
          "lines": [
            "SADD visitors:0907 u9   # exact, lists members",
            "SETBIT active:0907 9 1  # exact, dense int IDs",
            "PFADD hll:0907 u9       # approximate, 12 KB",
            "PFCOUNT hll:0907        # PFMERGE rolls up weeks"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pick HLL when you only need \"how many unique?\"",
            "Never need to list or test members — HLL cannot do either",
            "`SCARD` is exact but stores every element forever",
            "Bitmap is sized by max ID, not by member count",
            "Sparse IDs make a bitmap wasteful; dense IDs make it tiny"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Pipeline vs Transaction",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "diagram",
          "kind": "cards",
          "spec": {
            "cards": [
              {
                "title": "pipeline",
                "lines": [
                  "N commands, 1 RTT",
                  "not atomic",
                  "throughput lever",
                  "others interleave"
                ]
              },
              {
                "title": "MULTI/EXEC",
                "lines": [
                  "queued then serial",
                  "nothing interleaves",
                  "no rollback",
                  "still one RTT"
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Pipelining sends N commands without waiting for replies",
            "Biggest throughput lever there is, and it is not atomic",
            "`MULTI` queues, `EXEC` runs them with nothing interleaved",
            "A runtime error in one queued command does not undo the rest"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "redis",
          "lines": [
            "WATCH cart:42        # optimistic lock",
            "MULTI",
            "SADD cart:42 sku-9",
            "EXEC   # nil if cart:42 changed -> retry"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Server-Side Logic"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`EVAL script numkeys key ... arg ...` runs atomically",
            "`SCRIPT LOAD` then `EVALSHA` to skip resending the body",
            "Use for read-then-write logic that must not interleave",
            "`FUNCTION LOAD` + `FCALL` is the modern persisted replacement",
            "Keep scripts short — slow Lua blocks the server like any command"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "lua",
          "lines": [
            "if redis.call('GET', KEYS[1]) == ARGV[1] then",
            "  return redis.call('DEL', KEYS[1])",
            "end",
            "return 0  -- someone else holds the lock"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Distributed Locks, End to End",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "takeaway",
          "title": "Lock release",
          "text": "Acquire with `SET lock:<res> <token> NX EX 30`. Release must be that Lua compare-and-delete, or you will delete somebody else's lock."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Lock, End To End"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "redis",
          "lines": [
            "SET lock:ord:42 t9f NX EX 30",
            "# OK = held, nil = retry later",
            "EVALSHA <sha> 1 lock:ord:42 t9f"
          ],
          "note": "token must be unique per client"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Why not SETNX + EXPIRE",
          "items": [
            "Two commands, so not atomic",
            "Client dies between them → lock never expires",
            "`SETNX` and `SETEX` are legacy sugar",
            "`SET ... NX EX` does it in **one** command"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Expiry, Rate Limits & Eviction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "How Expiry Really Works"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Keys are removed lazily on access, plus an active sampling cycle",
            "An expired key can hold memory a while after its TTL passes",
            "Only top-level keys expire, aside from hash-field TTLs",
            "No expiring one list element or one set member",
            "Model that as a sorted set scored by time + `ZREMRANGEBYSCORE`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Rate Limiting, Two Ways"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "redis",
          "lines": [
            "INCR rl:u7:202609071803   # fixed window",
            "EXPIRE rl:u7:202609071803 60 NX",
            "ZREMRANGEBYSCORE rl:u7 -inf 1757000000",
            "ZCARD rl:u7               # then ZADD now"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Window",
            "Accuracy",
            "Memory"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "fixed / `INCR`",
              "2x burst at edge",
              "one counter"
            ],
            [
              "sliding / zset",
              "accurate",
              "one entry per hit"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "TTL Is A Key Property"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`EXPIRE key 60`, `TTL key`, `PERSIST key` removes it",
            "`TTL` returns -1 for no TTL, -2 for no key at all",
            "`SET` replaces the value and clears the TTL with it",
            "`APPEND`, `LPUSH`, `HSET`, `INCR` mutate in place and keep it",
            "`SET key v KEEPTTL` preserves the TTL through a replace"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Deleting safely",
          "items": [
            "`DEL key` is O(N) in elements freed",
            "`UNLINK key` frees in a background thread",
            "`UNLINK` returns immediately — prefer it on big keys"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Cache And Memory Policy"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Cache-aside: `GET`, on miss load DB, `SET key val EX ttl`",
            "Add random jitter to the TTL so the herd does not expire together",
            "`notify-keyspace-events` publishes expirations and evictions",
            "Delivery is fire-and-forget — it is not a queue",
            "`SCAN`/`SSCAN`/`HSCAN`/`ZSCAN` are the only safe walks in prod"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "maxmemory policies",
          "items": [
            "`noeviction`: writes fail with `OOM command not allowed`",
            "`allkeys-lru` / `allkeys-lfu`: evict anything",
            "`volatile-ttl`: evict the soonest to expire",
            "Choose deliberately — the default surprises people"
          ],
          "ink": "red"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Seven Mistakes, Durability & Debug Tools",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Seven Mistakes",
          "ink": "accent"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Big keys: millions of elements make every touch slow",
            "They block failover and cannot be sharded",
            "`KEYS *` in app code — the classic production stall",
            "Unbounded growth: no `LTRIM`, no `ZREMRANGEBYRANK`, no TTL",
            "No TTL on cache keys: they never leave, writes fail at `maxmemory`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Wrong type: `LINDEX` in a loop, `SMEMBERS` then client-side filter",
            "Wrong type: a JSON string rewritten for one field change",
            "N+1 round trips: N sequential `GET`s = N network RTTs",
            "Fix with `MGET`, a pipeline, or one Lua script"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "redis",
          "lines": [
            "# before: GET p:1, p:2, p:3",
            "# = 3 network round trips",
            "MGET p:1 p:2 p:3   # 1 RTT"
          ],
          "note": "cluster: keys must share a slot"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Durability Is A Choice"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Mode",
            "Loses"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "`RDB` snapshot",
              "since last save"
            ],
            [
              "`AOF everysec`",
              "~1 second"
            ],
            [
              "`AOF always`",
              "~nothing, slower"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "cloud",
          "title": "Cluster gotcha",
          "text": "Multi-key commands (`SINTER`, `MGET`, `ZUNIONSTORE`) need every key in the same hash slot. Force it with a hash tag: `{user1000}:profile`."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Found a big key?",
          "text": "Shard it into ==bucketed hashes==, cap it with `LTRIM`, and delete the old one with `UNLINK`, never `DEL`."
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Debug Toolkit"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "shell",
          "lines": [
            "redis-cli --bigkeys      # find the fat collections",
            "redis-cli --memkeys      # memory per key sample",
            "redis-cli --latency      # round-trip profile",
            "redis-cli MEMORY USAGE feed:42"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "page_title": "Slowlog & Symptom Triage",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`SLOWLOG GET` lists commands over `slowlog-log-slower-than`",
            "First place to look when latency spikes",
            "`OBJECT ENCODING key` confirms the compact encoding held",
